sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hamilton-haplodiploidy> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis>;
    dc:description "Because full sisters in haplodiploid species share three quarters of their genes, more than a mother shares with her offspring, haplodiploid insects are predisposed to evolve sterile worker castes.";
    dc:source "W. D. Hamilton (1964) The genetical evolution of social behaviour. II";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Haplodiploidy hypothesis for eusociality";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09205>, <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1156108>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "with a singly mated queen, r_{sister,sister} = 3/4 > r_{mother,daughter} = 1/2, but r_{sister,brother} = 1/4";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Formal status: the relatedness coefficients are proved. The explanatory claim is largely abandoned: the low sister-brother relatedness offsets the gain unless the sex ratio is biased, diploid termites are eusocial, and comparative work points to ancestral monogamy instead (Hughes et al. 2008).";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "conjecture";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Use phylogenetic comparative analysis to test whether eusociality arises more often in haplodiploid than in diploid lineages after controlling for ancestral mating system."
}
